WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.950
The writers behind Star Wars: The Rise of
Skywalker have got some explaining to do.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.240
Skywalker writer/director J.J. Abrams was
recently captured on video explaining to a

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.440
group of fans the oddly less-than-defined
relationship between Rey and Kylo Ren in the

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.150
Star Wars sequel films.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:19.970
Ever since the release of Star Wars: The Force
Awakens, there has been a significant subset

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:25.019
of fans call them the "Reylo contingent" who
have been pining to see the Resistance fighter

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:30.759
Rey and the First Order villain Kylo just
admit their feelings for each other, already.

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:34.720
There has always been a strong chemistry between
the two which could easily be taken for a

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.690
romantic undertone to their relationship despite
the fact that for most of the sequel trilogy,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.100
the two were playing for opposite teams.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.500
During the events of The Rise of Skywalker,
Ren is moved to discard his old lightsaber

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:52.179
and reclaim his original identity as Ben Solo,
son of Han and Leia and the former pupil of

00:00:52.179 --> 00:00:53.440
Luke Skywalker.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.309
After Rey is seemingly killed during her fight
with Emperor Palpatine, Ben redeems himself

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:02.089
by transferring his life force to her, saving
her life.

00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:06.939
Before he becomes one with the Force, he kisses
her, seemingly vindicating every fan who ever

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:10.040
insisted that there was something going on
between those two.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.460
"You come from nothing.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.799
You're nothing.

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:19.479
But not to me."

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:23.880
According to Abrams, though, that something
wasn't what we might have thought it was.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.730
His off-the-cuff explanation of their relationship
to a group of fans was posted online by Twitter

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:32.789
user @rizeofkylo, and it may make you cringe
just a little.

00:01:32.789 --> 00:01:34.990
In the video they posted, Abrams said,

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.740
"There's as much of a brother/sister thing
between Kylo and Rey as there is a romantic

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:45.270
thing...It's more like they're just bound
together in this crazy, spiritual way."

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:50.090
Abrams bolstered his argument by looking to
musical maestro John Williams, who composed

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.590
the music for Star Wars from the very beginning,
before things got so complicated between its

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:55.590
main characters.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.590
The filmmaker said,

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.149
"If you listen to John Williams when he first
wrote the Luke theme, it was a romantic theme

00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:02.429
for Luke and Leia.

00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:05.960
That was kind of what he was thinking, because
he didn't know where it was going."

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.940
"You and your brother aren't related, right?"

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.630
What some might find most interesting about
Abrams' remarks is how they counter a common

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.640
criticism of the Star Wars sequel trilogy.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.930
You'll recall that Abrams directed The Force
Awakens, then handed off The Last Jedi to

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:25.740
writer-director Rian Johnson; Abrams was then
recruited to finish off the trilogy after

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.480
the director who was originally attached to
Episode IX, Colin Trevorrow, departed due

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.340
to creative differences.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.590
It's also been noted that many of the controversial
narrative choices made by Johnson in The Last

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.490
Jedi were kind of undone by Abrams with The
Rise of Skywalker, like the issue of Rey's

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.300
parentage, for example.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.200
While some reports insist otherwise, it's
commonly understood that Disney began work

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.900
on the Star Wars sequel trilogy with not much
of an overarching plan for how the narrative

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:52.900
would go.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:57.010
Instead, the producers appear to have just
been kind of winging it, hoping that each

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.420
new filmmaker would seamlessly pick up from
where the last left off.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.650
Many fans have critiqued this way of doing
things, but in explaining Rey and Ren's relationship,

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.460
Abrams brings up an interesting point.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.240
The original Star Wars trilogy, those films
most beloved by the fandom, also didn't have

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.880
any kind of grand design in place from the
start.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.570
While series creator George Lucas later insinuated
that he always had a sweeping plan in place

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:25.360
for the saga, it's a matter of fact that many
of the original trilogy's key plot elements,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.290
such as Darth Vader being Luke's father, and
Luke and Leia being brother and sister, fell

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.410
into place pretty late in the game.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:36.930
This is why The Empire Strikes Back widely
considered to be the best movie in the franchise,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.570
and one of the greatest movies of all time,
deigned to give us the sight of Luke and Leia,

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:45.820
whom we would learn in the next movie were
not only brother and sister but twins, engaged

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.580
in a passionate smooch.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.250
"Oh my! Princess Leia!"

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:55.360
All of which is to say: look, fans, we can
all gripe about the sequels' narrative not

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.330
being consistent, but let's apply a little
consistency ourselves.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:03.210
The new flicks may have been a bit confused
about Rey and Ren's relationship, but at least

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.930
they never forced us to watch twin siblings
making out.

